<h2>Why one package works everywhere</h2>
<p>Everything the system <em>looks</em> like is CSS: <code>im-btn</code>, <code>im-card</code>, <code>im-cols</code>. Everything it <em>does</em> is a plain script that reads data attributes: <code>data-im-tabs</code>, <code>data-im-reveal</code>, <code>data-theme-toggle</code>. Nothing is written for one framework, so nothing has to be written again for the next. Handlebars appears only in the Ghost partials and in these docs, because Ghost renders Handlebars; a React or HTML project never sees it.</p>
<p>The scripts watch the page for new markup, so content that arrives later — a React render, a client-side route, a list that grows — is set up on its own. Each script runs once even if it is loaded twice.</p>

